        "Before choosing an asset, decide what job the money is supposed to do.\n1.1 Define the purpose and time horizon\nAsk yourself:\n- Is this money intended for a long-term goal?\n- When might I need to withdraw it?\n- Can I tolerate a temporary decline in value?\n- Would a permanent loss affect essential expenses?\n- Am I seeking growth, income, diversification, or preservation of purchasing power?\nThe source describes a long holding period of roughly two to ten years for its approach to individual stocks and discusses index investing over ten, twenty, or thirty years. These are examples of horizons, not universal rules. The appropriate time horizon depends on the goal and the risk of the asset.",
